// devanagari-demo — Devanagari (Hindi) typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/devanagari-demo.script
//
// Devanagari is written left to right, so it needs none of the bidirectional
// reordering Hebrew and Arabic do. What it needs instead is work inside each
// syllable. A consonant carries an inherent vowel; a following vowel sign (a
// matra) attaches above, below, before or after it; and a consonant joined to the
// next by the virama (the halant, U+094D) fuses into a conjunct or a half-form.
//
// The engine segments the run into orthographic syllables, then for each one asks
// the bundled Noto Serif Devanagari font's GSUB table to build the conjuncts and
// half-forms, reorders the short-i sign — typed after its consonant but drawn
// before the whole syllable — and asks GSUB again for the width-matched vowel
// variants. The below- and above-base signs are then placed with the same GPOS
// anchors the engine uses for Hebrew points and Arabic dots.
//
// \font devanagari selects the bundled face (also \font hindi), in a regular and a
// bold cut. No \rtl is needed — Devanagari is left to right.

\use{document}

\title{Devanagari in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Hindi paragraph}

Each syllable is shaped on its own and the syllables are set left to right. Within a
syllable the consonants may fuse and the vowel signs attach around the base; between
syllables the line breaker finds its breaks at the interword spaces exactly as for
Latin text.

\medskip

{\font devanagari 14 regular
हिन्दी भारत की एक प्रमुख भाषा है। यह देवनागरी लिपि में लिखी जाती है, जो बाईं ओर से दाईं ओर
लिखी जाती है। हर अक्षर एक व्यंजन और उसके साथ का स्वर मिलकर बनता है, और दो व्यंजन हलन्त
से जुड़कर संयुक्त अक्षर बनाते हैं।
}

\section{The short-i reordering}

The short-i sign is typed after its consonant but drawn before it. In each word below
the sign appears at the left of its syllable even though it follows the consonant in
memory --- heart, book, Hindi, and knowledge:

\medskip

{\font devanagari 16 regular
दिल   किताब   हिन्दी   विद्या
}

\section{Conjuncts and half-forms}

Two consonants joined by the virama fuse. Some become a single ligature glyph, others
a half-form of the first consonant set against the full second. The classics ---
kṣa, tra, jña, and the na-da half-form of \TeXish{}'s Hindi word:

\medskip

{\font devanagari 16 regular
क्ष   त्र   ज्ञ   न्द
}

\section{Reph and below-base ra}

The consonant \emph{ra} takes special forms when it joins another. A word-initial ra
followed by the virama becomes a reph --- a small hook drawn above the following base,
even though it is typed first --- as in these words for deed, duty, sun, and complete:

\medskip

{\font devanagari 16 regular
कर्म   धर्म   सूर्य   पूर्ण
}

\medskip

A ra after another consonant instead hangs below it, either fused into a single glyph
or set as a small stroke beneath the base:

\medskip

{\font devanagari 16 regular
प्र   क्र   द्र   ट्र   राष्ट्र
}

\section{Independent vowels and signs}

A vowel that opens a syllable is written with its own independent letter, not a matra.
The below-base and above-base signs stack onto their base:

\medskip

{\font devanagari 16 regular
अ आ इ ई उ ऊ ए ऐ ओ औ
}

\medskip

{\font devanagari 16 regular
कु कू के कै को कौ कं कः
}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font devanagari 18 bold
नमस्ते! यह हिन्दी में लिखा गया है।
}
